# Admin notices vs plugin hero

On WordPress admin screens for custom plugins (e.g. WPL / `wp-price-list`), **never place `admin_notices`, errors, or third-party notices inside the hero**.

## Required pattern

1. Open `.wrap` with a screen-reader `<h1>` + `<hr class="wp-header-end" />` **before** the hero so core JS relocates notices above it.
2. Put plugin toast/notices in a dedicated slot (e.g. `.wpl-notices`) **above** the hero.
3. Do **not** use a visible `<h1>` inside the hero — use a styled `<p>` / `<div>` for the display title (core moves notices after the first `h1`/`h2` in `.wrap`).
4. CSS fallback: hide `.notice` / `.e-notice` if they ever land inside the hero.

## Bad

```html
<header class="wpl-hero">
  <h1 class="wpl-hero__title">…</h1>
  <!-- WP injects Elementor/theme notices here -->
</header>
```

## Good

```html
<div class="wrap wpl-app">
  <h1 class="screen-reader-text">…</h1>
  <hr class="wp-header-end" />
  <div class="wpl-notices">…</div>
  <header class="wpl-hero">
    <p class="wpl-hero__title">…</p>
  </header>
</div>
```
